Object representing eventual completion or failure of async operation.

```javascript
const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve("done"), 1000);
});
promise.then(result => console.log(result));
```

Waits for all promises to resolve, returns array of results.

```javascript
Promise.all([promise1, promise2, promise3])
  .then(results => console.log(results));
// Rejects if any promise rejects
```

Waits for all promises to settle, returns array with status of each.

```javascript
Promise.allSettled([promise1, promise2])
  .then(results => console.log(results));
// [{status: "fulfilled", value: ...}, {status: "rejected", reason: ...}]
```

Syntactic sugar for working with Promises.

```javascript
async function fetchUser() {
  const response = await fetch('/api/user');
  const user = await response.json();
  return user;
}
```

Use try-catch blocks.

```javascript
async function fetchUser() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('/api/user');
    return await res.json();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
  }
}
```

Same functionality, different syntax. Async/await is more readable for sequential operations.

```javascript
// Promise
fetch('/api').then(res => res.json()).then(data => console.log(data));

// Async/await
const res = await fetch('/api');
const data = await res.json();
console.log(data);
```

Deeply nested callbacks making code hard to read.

```javascript
getData(function(a) {
  getMoreData(a, function(b) {
    getEvenMoreData(b, function(c) {
      // callback hell
    });
  });
});
```

SetTimeout with 0ms schedules execution after current call stack clears.

```javascript
console.log(1);
setTimeout(() => console.log(2), 0);
console.log(3);
// Output: 1, 3, 2
```

Wrap callback in Promise constructor.

```javascript
function readFilePromise(path) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    fs.readFile(path, (err, data) => {
      if (err) reject(err);
      else resolve(data);
    });
  });
}
```

No, forEach doesn't wait for async operations. Use for-of or Promise.all instead.

```javascript
// Wrong
items.forEach(async item => await process(item));

// Correct
for (const item of items) {
  await process(item);
}
```

- Parallel: operations run simultaneously
- Sequential: operations run one after another

```javascript
// Sequential
for (const url of urls) {
  await fetch(url);
}

// Parallel
await Promise.all(urls.map(url => fetch(url)));
```
